---
concept: wall
---
-- replay --
$ dorc explain --terse wall
A wall is a command in your book that Dorc knows nothing about: no fact
measured above it can be trusted below it, so the commands after a wall
are re-checked or run instead of being removed from the plan.

$ dorc explain --summary wall
When Dorc plans, it removes a command from your book only when it can
prove the command's work is already done. That proof is built from
read-only checks that run before anything else does, so it has a hidden
dependency: the world must hold still between the measurement and the
moment the line would have run. A command no oracle describes breaks that
stillness - Dorc cannot know what it will change, so everything measured
earlier might be stale by the time the lines below it are reached. Such a
command, when it is really going to run, is a wall: the lines above it
keep their proofs, and the lines below it fall back to being re-checked
in place, or simply run.

$ dorc explain wall
wall

Dorc removes a command from your plan only on proof: a read-only check
ran on the host, measured the command's work already done, and the
command's own oracle vouched that measuring-it-done is reason enough not
to run it. Every one of those measurements happens at planning time,
before anything runs. So every proof has a hidden dependency: the world
must hold still between the measurement and the moment the line would
have run.

Most of the time it does. The exception is a command Dorc knows nothing
about.

   > $ dorc explain --terse unmodeled
   > A command is unmodeled when no loaded oracle describes it. Dorc
   > treats it as capable of changing anything on the host, because in
   > Dorc, silence never grants permission.

An unmodeled command that is really going to run is called a wall. Dorc
cannot know what it touches, so it must assume it may touch anything -
including whatever some check above it measured. From the wall down,
planning-time proof is spent: a fact measured before the wall may be
stale after it.

What happens below a wall is a downgrade, not a failure. A line that
measured already-done cannot be removed from the plan, but it can be
guarded: its oracle's own check is placed directly in front of it,
re-verifying at run time, after the wall has done whatever it does. A
guarded line is fast and safe; it is just not gone. The cost of a wall
is paid in attention, not in correctness.

   > worked example (case plan-first-wall):
   > $ dorc plan --book=book.sh web1
   >  3  # apt-get update                  # converged: index fresh
   >  4  hork tune --profile web           # runs: unmodeled ('hork')
   >  5  ( systemctl_check enable nginx ) \
   >  5     || systemctl enable nginx      # verify: past 'hork' (line 4)
   > plan: 1 to run, 1 to verify (1 skipped)

Two things are worth reading off that render. The apt-get line, above
the wall, kept its proof and is gone. The systemctl line measured
already-done too - but 'hork' stands between the measurement and the
moment line 5 would run, so its proof does not carry, and the line stays
in the plan wearing a guard.

Walls fall one command at a time, and always by authorship. An oracle
describing 'hork' - even the two-minute kind that only answers "is this
already done?" - lets Dorc measure it, and a wall that measures
already-done is removed from the plan entirely. A command that will not
run cannot invalidate anything: a removed wall casts no shadow. This is
why describing the first wall in a book is the steepest part of the
value curve; it buys back its own line and every proof it was poisoning.

see also: dorc explain unmodeled; dorc explain guard;
dorc explain oracle; dorc explain plan-lifecycle
